.TH EVENT_LOOP_ADD 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
event_loop_add \- register a selectable with an event loop
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

event_reg event_loop_add(event_loop loop, ccol_selectable sel,
                       event_handlers_t handlers, void *arg,
                       char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Registers sel with loop so that its readiness is dispatched through
handlers for as long as the registration lives, or until
event_loop_remove() is called.

sel is built exactly the way ccol_select() builds one: selectable_from_fd,
selectable_from_circq, selectable_from_dynq, and selectable_from_chan are
all directly reusable.

One registration covers exactly one direction (sel.dir).  A caller
wanting both directions live on the same fd at once (e.g. a full-duplex
pipe being read and written concurrently) calls event_loop_add() twice
and gets two independent handles.  Flipping a single registration's
direction over time instead (e.g. a connecting socket: write-interest
until connect completes, then read-interest afterward) uses one
registration plus event_loop_modify(3).  Registering a second selectable
for a direction already occupied on the same fd (two read registrations
on one fd, for example) is rejected with ccol_not_permitted.

A queue/channel selectable has no such restriction: any number of
event_loop_add() calls for the same queue+direction (across one or more
event_loop instances), and any mix of those with a concurrent
ccol_select() call on the same queue+direction, are all live at once and
all eventually get a turn, mirroring ccol_select()'s own guarantee that
any number of threads may simultaneously watch the same queue.  A message
that arrives while more than one such listener is registered wakes
exactly one of them per producer/consumer call; whichever one is woken
checks, once its own callback returns, whether the queue is still ready
for its direction and, if so, forwards the wake to the next listener in
line, so no live listener is ever passed over indefinitely.  A backlog
deeper than the number of live listeners may still need a further,
unrelated send/receive to fully drain (see handlers.on_readable's own
note below on notifications not being guaranteed one-to-one with
messages).

A queue registered this way must outlive its registration:
circular_queue_destroy(3)/dynamic_queue_destroy(3)/channel_destroy(3)
assert if the queue still has a live registration (or an in-progress
ccol_select()/ccol_select_timed() call) watching it, since the
still-linked waiter would otherwise reference the queue's own,
about-to-be-freed mutex. Call event_loop_remove() before destroying the
queue; destroying the loop first is fine, since event_loop_destroy()/
event_loop_remove() always unlink a queue's waiter before it is ever
touched again.

handlers.on_readable reports readiness only, for every selectable type:
event_loop never performs the receive itself.  For fd selectables, the
callback performs its own read(2)/recv(2) on sel->fd.  For queue/channel
selectables, the callback performs its own circq_try_recv_zc()/
dynmq_try_recv_zc() on sel->cq/sel->dq.  Either way a concurrent consumer
may have already claimed the data (a TOCTOU race inherent to the design,
same as ccol_select()'s own write-direction contract); the callback's
own explicit receive call may find nothing, and must handle that
gracefully.  For a queue selectable specifically, a notification is not
guaranteed to correspond to exactly one message (several sends that
outrun dispatch can be observed as a single notification); a callback
that must not leave messages stranded under bursty traffic should call
circq_try_recv_zc()/dynmq_try_recv_zc() in a loop until it returns
ccol_container_empty.  handlers.on_writable fires with no payload for either kind
(caller performs its own write(2)/send(2), or its own non-blocking
try-send for queues).  handlers.on_error fires only for fd selectables,
on EPOLLERR/EPOLLHUP, whenever the corresponding direction has no
handler to hand the co-occurring condition to instead: a read
registration with on_readable set may receive on_readable instead of
on_error for the same EPOLLERR/EPOLLHUP event if data is also
available (EPOLLIN/EPOLLRDHUP and EPOLLERR/EPOLLHUP are not mutually
exclusive), and a write registration with on_writable set may likewise
receive on_writable instead if the fd is also writable (EPOLLOUT and
EPOLLERR/EPOLLHUP are not mutually exclusive either: a socket that has
entered an error state is reported writable too, since write(2) on it
returns immediately with an error rather than blocking).  Queue
selectables never produce an error condition.  Any of on_readable,
on_writable, and on_error may be NULL, in which case that class of
event is silently dropped for this registration.  A read registration
with on_readable
set to NULL (an "error only" reader, watching solely for the fd to
die) must genuinely never receive ordinary data with no accompanying
error: the fd remaining readable with real, undrained bytes and no
EPOLLERR/EPOLLHUP is then neither dispatched as readable (no
on_readable to call) nor as an error (no error condition exists), so
it keeps being reported ready on every poll cycle with nothing to show
for it, a silent, unbounded dispatch spin.  The identical hazard
applies to a write registration with on_writable set to NULL (an
"error only" writer): it must genuinely never become writable with no
accompanying error either.

handlers.on_removed, unlike the other three, is not tied to a readiness
event: it fires exactly once, asynchronously, once it is provably safe
to release arg (see event_loop_remove(3)'s own NOTES for the precise
guarantee and why event_loop_remove() itself cannot always provide it
synchronously). May be left NULL for a registration whose arg needs no
release notification.

Callbacks run on one of loop's own background reactor threads, never on
the calling thread.  A callback must not block.

With more than one reactor thread configured (see
event_loop_create_with_mprocs(3)'s num_reactor_threads), a single
registration's callback is still never invoked concurrently with
itself, and a read registration and a write registration sharing the
same fd are never invoked concurrently with each other either; a
callback pair that shares state across both directions of one fd (e.g.
one TLS connection object) needs no additional locking of its own on
that account. This does not extend to two entirely different fds:
callbacks for different registrations may run concurrently on
different reactor threads.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop      event_loop to register with.
sel       What to watch; see DESCRIPTION.
handlers  Callback bundle (individual callbacks may be NULL).
arg       Opaque pointer passed to every callback for this registration.
err_str   Optional pointer to receive an error string on failure.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new registration handle on success.
Returns EVENT_REG_INVALID on failure: loop is EVENT_LOOP_INVALID or a
stale/already-destroyed handle, sel is malformed (invalid type/dir, NULL
queue pointer, or negative fd), the direction is already occupied on
that fd (ccol_not_permitted), or allocation/epoll_ctl failure.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
event_reg, like event_loop itself, is an opaque VALUE handle, not a
pointer: never cast it to/from void*, compare it via a pointer cast, or
treat it as an address. Compare it against EVENT_REG_INVALID (or use a
truthiness check; EVENT_REG_INVALID is 0). Every use of an event_reg is
resolved against its own loop's registration table before anything is
dereferenced, so a stale or already-removed handle is always detected
and rejected cleanly rather than risking a use-after-free, even when
racing a concurrent event_loop_modify/_pause/_resume/_remove/
event_loop_reg_generation call on the very same registration from
another thread.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call concurrently with event_loop_remove(), event_loop_modify(),
and from within a callback running on the reactor thread.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void on_msg(event_loop loop, ccol_selectable *sel, void *arg) {
    c_message_t msg;
    if (circq_try_recv_zc(sel->cq, &msg) != ccol_success) return;
    printf("job: %.*s\en", (int)msg.size, (char *)msg.data);
    free(msg.data);
}

circular_queue *jobs = circular_queue_create(64, NULL);
event_handlers_t handlers = { .on_readable = on_msg };
event_reg reg = event_loop_add(loop, selectable_from_circq(jobs, ccol_select_read),
                               handlers, NULL, NULL);
.fi
